Thank you for implementing this! I have tested it with opencode 1.2.10.

**Working config format:**
```json
{
  "compaction": {
    "token_threshold": 60000,
    "context_threshold": 0.45,
    "models": {
      "google/antigravity-gemini-3-flash": {
        "context_threshold": 0.30
      },
      "google/antigravity-gemini-3-pro-high": {
        "context_threshold": 0.30
      }
    }
  }
}
```

**Note:** The following keys from my previous config are **not supported** in this implementation:
- `auto`, `prune`, `reserved`, `strategy`, `threshold`, `preserveRecentMessages`, `preserveSystemPrompt`

These should be removed. The supported keys are only:
- `token_threshold` (global default)
- `context_threshold` (global default)
- `models` (per-model overrides with same keys)

**Feature request:** Would it be possible to add `min_messages` (minimum messages between compactions) to prevent continuous compaction loops? This would be especially useful for long-running sessions.

@MonsieurTib 
To answer your question, ripgrep is a high-performance implementation of a grep-like search tool used to search for patterns across a codebase and its files. OpenCode uses regrep internally because it is lightweight and optimized for speed and efficiency. The tools used for searching within codebases in OpenCode rely on ripgrep, as documented in their internals documentation: https://opencode.ai/docs/tools/#internals

• Whether this ripgrep scan is expected behavior - Yes it is based on the docs, its how the agent finds things in the codebase
• What feature triggers this search - When ever the model needs to run a search of the codebase for a pattern of text it makes use of it. For example you talk about needing it to check a function only providing the function name, it uses regrep to find where that function name is used or written in the whole codebase.
• Why it may run while the system is idle - It ideally wont run if not requested, it is like a tool and only used when run by the agent itself.
• Whether background scans can be disabled - Regrep when used through a tool is not able to search outside the directory that you have opencode running in, expect the user provides permission for [external_directories] to be accessed(https://opencode.ai/docs/permissions/#external-directories) or when prompted for permission granted the agent permission to search outside the folder/project opencode was run in. But to prevent running of the command itself users can add permissions for the `rg` command making sure it either always asks or gets denyed https://opencode.ai/docs/permissions#granular-rules-object-syntax
• Whether this behavior is documented - Yes again its talked about in the specific tools that make use of it, and also ways to limit its search on specific files https://opencode.ai/docs/tools/#internals and here is a link to the ripgrep project https://github.com/BurntSushi/ripgrep

What I can advise is to add a permission rule that forces confirmation (or denial) if a direct rg command on the bash tool is attempted. This discourages bash use and keeps searches within the more constrained built‑in opencode tools like `grep`, `glob`, `list`.

```json
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "permission": {
    "bash": {
      "rg *": "ask"
    },
  }
}
```

